  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, preferring the one that is more left-skewed. However, as established, neither lottery exhibits meaningful skewness; both are symmetric or certain. The individual did not refer to or consider distribution shape, asymmetry, or any concept resembling skewness. Instead, the individual focused narrowly on the 50% risk of getting 0 Euros in Lottery B. While this consideration may align indirectly with preferring more probability on better outcomes (a loose interpretation of left-skewness), the reasoning process described does not reflect an evaluation of skewness as a structural property of the lotteries. The justification is based on risk aversion to a zero outcome, not on comparative skewness. Therefore, although there is a superficial alignment in preferring A due to higher probability on a favorable outcome, the cognitive process described does not match the decision reason.",
